1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,454
A smoother opening price can still give early buyers more tokens.

2
00:00:04,404 --> 00:00:08,166
Curve Covenant exposes both outcomes before launch.

3
00:00:08,166 --> 00:00:13,952
This recording uses a local validator, synthetic Xerox tokens, and a test signer.

4
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:21,225
Both curves use the same supply, opening price, quote reserve threshold, and fee schedule.

5
00:00:21,175 --> 00:00:29,128
In this whale scenario, the long curve reduces the first price jump from three hundred eleven percent to two hundred thirty nine percent.

6
00:00:29,128 --> 00:00:34,830
But the early cohort receives two point five three percentage points more of total buy output.

7
00:00:34,830 --> 00:00:37,413
The ending prices also differ.

8
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,788
Switch to selling pressure.

9
00:00:40,738 --> 00:00:45,452
An opening buyer sells half their tokens, followed by retail buying.

10
00:00:45,452 --> 00:00:50,321
Every event updates price, reserves, fees, and inventory.

11
00:00:50,321 --> 00:00:54,428
The report measures buying needed to recover the pre sale price.

12
00:00:54,428 --> 00:00:56,964
Neither curve graduates here.

13
00:00:58,166 --> 00:01:02,877
Export the integer ledger as JSON, or download a visual summary.

14
00:01:02,827 --> 00:01:05,435
Select the long curve for launch.

15
00:01:05,435 --> 00:01:09,768
The exact compared configuration goes to the transaction builder.

16
00:01:09,768 --> 00:01:13,518
A versioned share link restores that same configuration.

17
00:01:14,699 --> 00:01:18,946
The sixteen segment configuration needs two transactions.

18
00:01:18,896 --> 00:01:23,694
The test signer confirms them, and the application reads the created pool.

19
00:01:23,694 --> 00:01:28,229
This verifies application wiring against a real local validator.

20
00:01:28,229 --> 00:01:32,099
It is not a Phantom extension or mainnet demonstration.

21
00:01:33,299 --> 00:01:37,249
Read wallet balances, then request a small buy quote.

22
00:01:37,199 --> 00:01:40,163
Review the minimum output before signing.

23
00:01:40,163 --> 00:01:46,508
After confirmation, the application refreshes both balances from the token accounts used for trading.

24
00:01:47,699 --> 00:01:50,518
Sell half the tokens just received.

25
00:01:50,468 --> 00:01:56,991
The input uses base token precision, and the transaction enforces the displayed minimum quote output.

26
00:01:56,991 --> 00:02:01,694
Confirmation decreases the base balance and increases the quote balance.

27
00:02:02,898 --> 00:02:06,371
A further buy reaches the threshold with a partial fill.

28
00:02:06,321 --> 00:02:09,667
Migration creates the DAMM version two pool.

29
00:02:09,667 --> 00:02:14,714
The application verifies the destination and displays both vault balances.

30
00:02:14,714 --> 00:02:17,214
These are local transactions.

31
00:02:18,398 --> 00:02:26,669
Validation covered fifty six swaps across eight local pools: SOL and Xerox, both curves, fixed and declining fees.

32
00:02:26,619 --> 00:02:33,178
Predicted raw prices, reserves, fees, inventory, and quote transfers matched execution.

33
00:02:33,178 --> 00:02:36,464
Complete data and hashes are in the repository.

34
00:02:37,631 --> 00:02:42,795
These scenarios expose trade offs, not forecasts or a universal winner.

35
00:02:42,745 --> 00:02:47,042
External quote liquidity and stock price changes are not modeled.

36
00:02:47,042 --> 00:02:50,804
Public Phantom evidence and builder trials remain next.

37
00:02:50,804 --> 00:02:54,042
Explore the product and reproducible source.
